  "text": "**Solution 1 is the more likely correct answer, $f(n)=\\frac{n+1}{3}$, though I could not verify it against the source.**\n\n**Solution 2's lower-bound construction is invalid.** It takes a blow-up of a directed 3-cycle, with complete bipartite joins $V_1\\to V_2\\to V_3\\to V_1$. That graph contains triangles: any $a\\in V_1$, $b\\in V_2$, $c\\in V_3$ are pairwise adjacent. The problem restricts to triangle-free graphs, so the construction does not apply. Its citation is also doubtful: the Czygrinow–Molla–Nagle–Oursler result concerns even rainbow cycles in general edge-coloured graphs, not triangle-free ones. Without that construction, the claimed sharpness of $(n+5)/3$ is unsupported.\n\n**Solution 1 is consistent with what I recall.** I believe the result is that for large $n$, every edge-coloured triangle-free graph with $\\delta^c(G)\\ge (n+1)/3$ contains a rainbow $C_4$, and this is sharp. That would sharpen the earlier $\\delta^c > n/3+1$ bound of Čada, Kaneko, Ryjáček and Yoshimoto. I have not checked the sharpness construction, so this rests on memory rather than a verified proof.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
